multipliers with more than one unit-cell cycle: {3: [(1, 0, 1, 0), (1, 1, -1, -1), (2, 1, 1, 1), (3, 2, -1, -5)], 5: [(1, 0, 1, 0), (2, 1, -1, -1)], 7: [(1, 0, 1, 0), (3, 1, 1, 1)], 9: [(1, 0, 1, 0), (3, 1, -1, -1)], 15: [(1, 0, 1, 0), (4, 1, 1, 1)], 17: [(1, 0, 1, 0), (4, 1, -1, -1)], 31: [(1, 0, 1, 0), (5, 1, 1, 1)], 33: [(1, 0, 1, 0), (5, 1, -1, -1)], 63: [(1, 0, 1, 0), (6, 1, 1, 1)], 65: [(1, 0, 1, 0), (6, 1, -1, -1)], 127: [(1, 0, 1, 0), (7, 1, 1, 1)], 129: [(1, 0, 1, 0), (7, 1, -1, -1)]}
  -> agrees with the researcher's list.  Prior art: the q = 181 cycle 27 -> 611 -> 27 is in Crandall
