4. Universal eigenvalues, exactly: gcd over several primes of the characteristic
   polynomials of 2K^- = R_e - R_o and 2K = R_e + R_o  (sympy, over Q)
==============================================================================
  q =   3: gcd charpoly(2K^-) = x**2*(x + 1);   gcd charpoly(2K) = x - 2
  q =   5: gcd charpoly(2K^-) = x**2;   gcd charpoly(2K) = x - 2
  q =   7: gcd charpoly(2K^-) = x**2;   gcd charpoly(2K) = x - 2
  q =   9: gcd charpoly(2K^-) = x**3;   gcd charpoly(2K) = x - 2
  q =  15: gcd charpoly(2K^-) = x**2;   gcd charpoly(2K) = x - 2
  q =  17: gcd charpoly(2K^-) = x**2;   gcd charpoly(2K) = x - 2
  q =  31: gcd charpoly(2K^-) = x**2;   gcd charpoly(2K) = x - 2
  q =  33: gcd charpoly(2K^-) = x**2;   gcd charpoly(2K) = x - 2
  (eigenvalue of K = root / 2.)  q = 3: the common factor of 2K^- contains (x + 1), i.e. -1/2;
  for the other q only powers of x (and x - 2, i.e. eigenvalue 1 of K) are common.  Agrees.

