5. NEW (skeptic): the functional exists for every word in a unit cell with D = -1, and for
   no word in a unit cell with D = +1.
   Two-branch signed kernel  (K^-_w f)(x) = ( f(x/2^k) - f(T_w(x)) ) / 2,  T_w(x) = (N x + C)/2^k,
   N = 3^s.  Let x_w = C/(2^k - N) (fixed point of T_w), a = -C/(N - 1) (where the branches agree).
   Then x_w/2^k = a  <=>  (2^k - 1)(2^k + 1 - N) = 0  <=>  N = 2^k + 1  <=>  D = -1,
   and in that case (delta_a - delta_{x_w}) K^-_w = -(1/2)(delta_a - delta_{x_w}),
   a non-zero functional for every prime p not dividing 6*C*(2^k - 1).
==============================================================================
  q=3 cell (1,1) word 1   (3 = 2+1), x_w = -1: D = -1; x_w/2^k == a: True;  -1/2 in spectrum at 44 of 44 primes
  q=3 cell (3,2) word 110 (9 = 8+1), x_w = -5: D = -1; x_w/2^k == a: True;  -1/2 in spectrum at 42 of 42 primes;  degenerate primes p | C(2^k-1) (p, eigenvalue present?): [(5, False), (7, False)]
  q=3 cell (3,2) word 101,           x_w = -7: D = -1; x_w/2^k == a: True;  -1/2 in spectrum at 43 of 43 primes;  degenerate primes p | C(2^k-1) (p, eigenvalue present?): [(7, False)]
  q=3 cell (3,2) word 011,           x_w = -10: D = -1; x_w/2^k == a: True;  -1/2 in spectrum at 42 of 42 primes;  degenerate primes p | C(2^k-1) (p, eigenvalue present?): [(5, False), (7, False)]
  q=3 cell (2,1) word 10  (3 = 4-1), x_w = +1: D = +1; x_w/2^k == a: False;  -1/2 in spectrum at 9 of 44 primes
  q=3 cell (2,1) word 01,            x_w = +2: D = +1; x_w/2^k == a: False;  -1/2 in spectrum at 9 of 44 primes
  q=5 cell (2,1) word 10  (5 = 4+1), x_w = -1: D = -1; x_w/2^k == a: True;  -1/2 in spectrum at 43 of 43 primes
  q=7 cell (3,1) word 100 (7 = 8-1), x_w = +1: D = +1; x_w/2^k == a: False;  -1/2 in spectrum at 18 of 43 primes
  q=3 cell (11,7) word of -17 (D = -139): D = -139; x_w/2^k == a: False;  -1/2 in spectrum at 3 of 40 primes;  degenerate primes p | C(2^k-1) (p, eigenvalue present?): [(17, False), (23, False), (89, False)]
  q=3 cell (5,3) word 11100 (D = +5), x_w = 19/5: D = +5; x_w/2^k == a: False;  -1/2 in spectrum at 0 of 40 primes;  degenerate primes p | C(2^k-1) (p, eigenvalue present?): [(19, False), (31, False)]
  (Primes dividing C*(2^k - 1) are set aside: there x_w = a mod p and the functional vanishes.)
  So the repo's 'q = d + 1' criterion (memory note AZ'': pairs (3,2), (5,4), (9,8), (17,16) ...)
  is exactly 'the one-word cell is a unit cell with D = -1'.  Of the three non-trivial unit cells
  of q = 3, the two with D = -1, (1,1) and (3,2), carry the functional, anchored at the negative
  cycles -1 and -5; the D = +1 cell (2,1) (cycle {1,2}) does not.  Residue-only statement:
  it sees 9 = 8 + 1, not the sign of the integers (3x-1 has the same kernels up to x -> -x).
